<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Palaver MCP server (LaunchAgent) — task 6.5.

  TEMPLATE. `palaver install-agent` with the mcp service selected substitutes
  every dollar-brace placeholder below and writes the result to
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/. Do not load this file directly; launchd would
  reject the unsubstituted XML.

  This comment names no placeholder literally, because string.Template treats
  one inside a comment exactly as it treats one inside a value. It also names
  no command-line flag literally: a double hyphen is illegal inside an XML
  comment, and `plutil -lint` accepts one anyway while expat and plistlib
  reject the file outright.

  A near-copy of observe.plist.tmpl, and deliberately a copy rather than a
  shared template with the scheduling keys parameterized: the difference
  between the two is that this job *omits* LowPriorityIO and Nice entirely,
  and a placeholder can substitute a value but cannot remove an element.
  Rendering <false/> instead would not be the same thing — it is a claim
  about priority rather than the absence of one — and injecting raw XML
  through a placeholder would bypass the escaping render_plist exists to do.
  tests/test_supervision.py asserts the two templates agree on every key
  except the scheduling ones, so this copy cannot drift unnoticed.

  This supervises `palaver mcp`, the Streamable HTTP read surface other
  coding agents query. It holds a mode=ro SQLite connection and posts its two
  writes to the observer daemon's socket, so it is not this machine's writer
  and may be restarted independently of one (INV-2, and task 6.3).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>${LABEL}</string>

	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
${PROGRAM_ARGUMENTS}
	</array>

	<!--
	  KeepAlive is unconditional, for a different reason than the observer's.
	  The observer restarts on any exit because nothing else writes; this one
	  restarts on any exit because a client's registered URL is fixed in
	  ~/.claude.json and a dead listener there is not an error the agent can
	  route around — it is a tool that silently stopped existing.
	-->
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<true/>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  Stated explicitly at launchd's own default rather than left implicit:
	  the restart window asserted by tests/test_supervision.py is derived from
	  this number, and a window derived from an undeclared default would start
	  failing silently if the default ever moved.
	-->
	<key>ThrottleInterval</key>
	<integer>${THROTTLE_INTERVAL}</integer>

	<!--
	  Standard, and NOT Background — the one place this job's scheduling
	  differs from the observer's, and the reason it needs its own template.

	  The observer is Background because its work is nobody's request: it
	  ticks on a timer whether or not anyone is waiting. This process is the
	  opposite. Every cycle it burns is inside an agent's blocking tool call,
	  with a human watching that agent wait. launchd.plist(5) describes
	  Background's resource limits as existing "to prevent them from
	  disrupting the user experience", which is precisely the throttling this
	  job must not get: an agent asking Palaver a question is the user
	  experience.

	  Adaptive is the value that looks right here and is not. It moves a job
	  between Background and Interactive based on *XPC* transaction activity
	  (see xpc_transaction_begin(3)). This server speaks Streamable HTTP over
	  a TCP socket and never opens an XPC transaction, so launchd would see no
	  activity to promote on and the job would sit in Background permanently —
	  strictly worse than saying Background outright, because it would read
	  like it had been thought about.

	  Interactive is refused by the same man page unless responsiveness
	  "depends on it, and cannot be made Adaptive". A local read answered in
	  milliseconds does not clear that bar. Standard — launchd's own default
	  scheduling, no throttle, no elevated claim — is what is left, and it is
	  the correct answer rather than the residual one.

	  LowPriorityIO and Nice are absent for the same reason, not merely unset:
	  this job's I/O is a SQLite read an agent is blocked on.
	-->
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Standard</string>

	<!--
	  launchd starts jobs in `/`. The server anchors its own log file to the
	  project root by module path, but its stdout/stderr are launchd's to
	  place, and relative paths here would land in the filesystem root.
	-->
	<key>WorkingDirectory</key>
	<string>${WORKING_DIRECTORY}</string>

	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>${STDOUT_PATH}</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>${STDERR_PATH}</string>
</dict>
</plist>
